REGIONAL REGULATION OF MUNA REGENCY NUMBER 7 OF 2019

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE IN MUNA REGENCY

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF MUNA.

Considering:

a. that discrimination and violence against women and children constitute violations of human rights and crimes against humanity;
b. that in order to fulfill the constitutional rights of women and children and improve the quality of life, it is necessary to regulate the duties, authorities and responsibilities of the Regional Government in the protection of women and children victims of violence;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, and letter b, it is deemed necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and children victims of violence in Muna Regency.

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
3. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning the stipulation of government regulation in lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 Concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 237, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5946);
5. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
6. Law Number 13 of 2006 Concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia number 4635) as amended by Law Number 31 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 13 of 2006 Concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia number 5602);
7. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
8. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
9. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as last amended by Law Number 9 of 2015 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5659);
10. Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
11. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards in the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Empowerment of Child Victims of Violence. 14. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 80 of 2015 concerning the Formulation of Regional Legal Products, as amended by Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 120 of 2018 concerning Amendment to Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 80 of 2015 concerning the Formulation of Regional Regulations;

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF MUNA REGENCY

and

REGENT OF MUNA

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE IN MUNA REGENCY

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection of women and child victims of violence is carried out based on the principles of:
a. respect for human rights;
b. gender equality and justice;
c. non-discriminatory;
d. protection of victims' rights;
e. legal certainty.

Article 3

1. The objectives of protection of women and child victims of violence are to:
a. prevent acts of violence against women and children including human trafficking;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. provide services to women and child victims of acts of violence, reporters and witnesses;
e. facilitate and mediate domestic disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.
2. Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. economic neglect;
e. restriction of movement.

CHAPTER III

VICTIMS' RIGHTS

Article 4

Every woman and child victim of violence has the right to obtain:
a. respect for their dignity as a human being;
b. protection from family, community, local government and/or other parties either temporarily or based on government determination, protection from the court;
c. health services according to medical needs;
d. special handling related to victim confidentiality;
e. assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
f. spiritual guidance services;
g. determine their own decisions;

CHAPTER IV

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 5

Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection to women and children victims of violence are the shared responsibility of:
a. local government and village government;
b. community;
c. family;
d. parents.

Article 6

1. The Local Government is obligated and responsible for providing protection to women and children victims of violence.
2. The obligations and responsibilities of the Local Government as referred to in Article 5 letter a include:
a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children victims of violence based on Laws and Regulations;
b. establishing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women and children victims of violence;
c. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children victims of violence;
d. facilitating assistance, legal aid and legal services according to the needs of victims;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from victims of violence according to regional financial capacity; and
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children victims of violence.
3. The obligations of the Village Government as referred to in Article 5 letter a include:
a. establishing policies for the protection of women and children victims of violence, such as Village Regulations on the Protection of Women and Children or Village Head Regulations;
b. including programs for the protection of women and children in the Village Medium-Term Development Plan;
c. allocating budget for activities for the implementation of protection of women and children in the Village Revenue and Expenditure Budget;
d. establishing village posts for integrated services for women and children victims of violence;
e. providing service programs for women and children victims of violence in the village;
f. appointing administrators and victim assistance officers for village posts;
g. conducting prevention activities in the village;
h. conducting coordination and cooperation with related parties including the community, community-based services and the business sector;
i. conducting guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children at the village level.
4. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in one regional action plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus Organizations in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
5. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (4) is part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).

Article 7

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 5 letter b, are carried out in the form of community participation.
2. Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. preventing the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children;
b. providing information and/or reporting acts of violence against women and children to law enforcement or authorized parties;
c. participating in the handling of victims of acts of violence;
d. providing protection to victims;
e. providing emergency assistance.

CHAPTER V

PREVENTION OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 9

1. Prevention is intended to reduce potential risks and vulnerability of women and children from all forms of violence both occurring in the domestic and public sphere, including preventing recurrence of violence experienced by victims.
2. Prevention of violence against women and children includes:
a. prevention of violence in the family and community environment including villages;
b. prevention of violence in the educational environment;
c. prevention of violence in the workplace environment; and
d. prevention of violence in public transportation
3. Prevention of violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (2) may be carried out in the form of:
a. socialization especially to vulnerable groups;
d. establishing village posts for integrated services for women and children victims of violence;
e. providing service programs for women and children victims of violence in the village;
f. appointing administrators and victim assistance officers for village posts;
g. conducting prevention activities in the village;
h. conducting coordination and cooperation with related parties including the community, community-based services and the business sector;
i. conducting guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children at the village level.
4. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in one regional action plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus Organizations in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
5. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (4) is part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).

Article 7

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 5 letter b, are carried out in the form of community participation.
2. Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. preventing the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children;
b. providing information and/or reporting acts of violence against women and children to law enforcement or authorized parties;
c. participating in the handling of victims of acts of violence;
d. providing protection to victims;
e. providing emergency assistance.

CHAPTER V

PREVENTION OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 9

1. Prevention is intended to reduce potential risks and vulnerability of women and children from all forms of violence whether occurring in the domestic or public sphere, including preventing recurrence of violence experienced by victims.
2. Prevention of violence against women and children includes:
a. prevention of violence in the family and community environment including villages;
b. prevention of violence in the educational environment;
c. prevention of violence in the workplace environment; and
d. prevention of violence in public transportation
3. Prevention of violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (2) may be carried out in the form of:
a. socialization especially to vulnerable groups;
b. publication, cultural performances, youth jamborees and campaigns;
c. training:d. forming and facilitating groups or forums for children, adolescents, women and the community;
e. encouraging/making educational institutions violence-free zones against women and children;
f. establishing child-friendly regencies.
4. Prevention of violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (2) letter a is the responsibility of Regional Apparatus Organizations in charge of family affairs, community and village empowerment, information affairs and those in charge of women's empowerment and child protection affairs.
5. Prevention of violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (2) letter b is the responsibility of Regional Apparatus Organizations in charge of education affairs, including religious education.
6. Prevention of violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (2) letter c is the responsibility of Regional Apparatus Organizations in charge of labor affairs, trade and industry affairs, and small and medium enterprises and cooperatives affairs.
7. Prevention of violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (2) letter d is the responsibility of Regional Apparatus Organizations in charge of market affairs, public works affairs, information affairs and transportation affairs.
8. Implementation of prevention of violence against women and children is carried out by involving community organizations, non-governmental organizations, legal aid institutions, business sector, communities, children and adolescent groups, mass media and other related parties.

CHAPTER VI

SERVICES

Part One Forms of Services

Article 10

1. Forms of services to women and children victims of violence include:
a. complaint services;
b. medical services;
c. psychological services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. law enforcement and legal aid services;
f. repatriation and social reintegration services; and
g. assistance services;

Article 11

1. Complaint services as referred to in Article 10 letter a, include;
a. services providing information regarding rights and services provided to victims;
b. toll-free complaint telephone services;
c. outreach services; and
d. consultation services.
2. Complaint services as referred to in paragraph (1) are the responsibility of Regional Apparatus Organizations in charge of women's empowerment and child protection affairs.

Article 12

1. Medical services as referred to in Article 10 letter b, include:
a. consultation services;
b. emergency services;
c. examination services;
d. treatment and care services, both outpatient and inpatient;
e. referral services; and
f. medical services related to law such as visum and DNA tests.
2. Medical services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out through:
a. special mechanisms at emergency installations;
b. special medical personnel who have been trained in medical services for women and children victims of violence;
c. coordination with Integrated Service Centers, Regional Apparatus Organizations, law enforcement officials, victim assistance institutions and other related parties.
3. Medical services as referred to in paragraph (1) are the responsibility of Regional Apparatus Organizations in charge of health affairs.
4. Costs of medical services as referred to in paragraph (1) are the responsibility of the Regional Government.

Article 13

1. Psychological services as referred to in Article 10 letter c, include;
a. counseling services;
b. psychological examination services;
c. clinical psychological services; and
d. psychiatric services.
2. Psychological services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by special personnel who have been trained.
3. Psychological services as referred to in paragraph (1) are the responsibility of Regional Apparatus Organizations in charge of health affairs and women's empowerment and child protection affairs and may cooperate with professional organizations and other related parties.

Article 14

1. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 10 letter d, include;
a. shelter or safe house services;
b. assistance services by social workers;
c. peer group services; and
d. victim's family strengthening services.
2. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by special personnel who have been trained.
3. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) are the responsibility of Regional Apparatus Organizations in charge of social affairs.

Article 15

1. Law enforcement and legal aid services as referred to in Article 10 letter e, include:
a. legal consultation services;
b. assistance by legal counsel;
c. temporary protection from the police against threats and retaliation experienced by victims;
d. safe house protection from the Witness and Victim Protection Agency and/or Integrated Service Center;
e. assistance at every legal process from victim assistance institutions and/or from family; and
f. assistance of language experts or interpreters including sign language;
2. Law enforcement and legal aid services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by personnel who have been trained.
3. Law enforcement and legal aid services as referred to in paragraph (1) are the responsibility of Regional Apparatus Organizations in charge of legal affairs and those in charge of women's empowerment and child protection affairs which in their implementation may cooperate with advocate professional institutions, legal aid organizations, victim assistance institutions, law enforcement agencies, Regional Office of Law and Human Rights and other related parties.

Article 16

1. Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in article 10 letter f include:
a. repatriation and/or pick-up services;
b. population document services such as birth certificates, identity cards and family cards;
c. care services;
d. school scholarship provision services;
e. skills improvement and economic empowerment services;
f. family strengthening and/or counseling services;
g. community or educational institution organizer strengthening and/or counseling services; and
h. social protection program provision services in the fields of education, health, economy, social and housing.
2. Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) are the responsibility of Regional Apparatus Organizations in charge of social affairs, education affairs, health affairs, settlement and public works affairs, labor affairs and cooperatives and SME affairs, community and village empowerment affairs, trade and industry affairs, and population affairs.
3. Implementation of repatriation and reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) may cooperate with the business sector, zakat agencies, community organizations, non-governmental organizations and other related parties.

Article 17

1. Assistance services as referred to in Article 10 letter g include:
a. assistance services at hospitals or other medical service institutions;
b. assistance services at law enforcement institutions;
c. assistance services at government institutions and social institutions;
d. peer group services.
2. Assistance services as referred to in paragraph (1) are the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus Organization in charge of women's empowerment and child protection affairs.
3. Implementation of assistance services may be carried out in cooperation with social organizations, non-governmental organizations, victim assistance institutions and universities.

Article 21

1. Integrated services for the protection of women and children (PPTPPA) at the regency level are carried out by the Regency Integrated Service Center.
2. The establishment and facilitation of the operation of the Integrated Service Center for the protection of women and children (PPTPPA) at the regency level is the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus Organization in charge of women's empowerment and child protection affairs.
3. The Integrated Service Center for the protection of women and children (PPTPPA) as referred to in paragraph (1) is established with the tasks of:
a. providing victim services in an integrated and comprehensive manner;
b. providing complaint services integrated with assistance, medical and legal services;
c. making case handling referrals in accordance with the services needed by victims;
d. coordinating and cooperating in case handling with related parties;
e. collecting data on case handling;
f. providing various information needed by victims and the community;
g. preventing cases of violence against women and children.
4. The Integrated Service Center for the protection of women and children (PPTPPA) as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of;
a. related regional apparatus organizations;
b. government and/or private hospitals;
c. police;
d. district attorney's office;
e. district court;
f. advocates;
g. religious organizations;
h. community organizations;
i. professional organizations;
j. family empowerment and welfare;
k. victim assistance institutions; and
l. non-governmental organizations.
5. The structure of the Integrated Service Center for the protection of women and children (PPTPPA) as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
a. advisors;
b. chairperson;
c. secretary;
d. service secretariat;
e. divisions.
6. The Integrated Service Center for the protection of women and children (PPTPPA) as referred to in paragraph (1) is established through a regent's decree.

Article 22

1. Integrated services for the protection of women and children (PPTPPA) at the sub-district level are carried out by the Sub-district Integrated Service Center.
2. The establishment and facilitation of the operation of the Integrated Service Center for the protection of women and children (PPTPPA) at the sub-district level is the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus Organization in charge of women's empowerment and child protection affairs and the governance section.
3. The Integrated Service Center for the protection of women and children (PPTPPA) as referred to in paragraph (1) is established with the tasks of:
a. providing victim services in an integrated and comprehensive manner at the sub-district level;
b. providing complaint services;
c. making case handling referrals in accordance with the services needed by victims to the Regency Integrated Service Center and related parties at the sub-district level;
d. coordinating and cooperating in case handling with related parties at the sub-district level;
e. collecting data on case handling at the sub-district level;
f. providing various information needed by victims and the community at the sub-district level;
g. preventing cases of violence against women and children at the sub-district level.
4. The Integrated Service Center for the protection of women and children (PPTPPA) as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
a. sub-district government;
b. technical implementing units of agencies in the sub-district;
c. sector police;
d. community health centers;
e. family empowerment and welfare;
f. community organizations;
g. religious organizations.
5. The structure of the Integrated Service Center for the protection of women and children (PTPPA) as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
a. advisors;
b. chairperson;
c. secretary;
d. service secretariat;
e. divisions.
6. The structure of the Integrated Service Center for the protection of women and children (PTPPA) as referred to in paragraph (5) is established through a decree of the head of the Regional Apparatus Organization in charge of women's empowerment and child protection affairs.

Article 23

1. Integrated services for the protection of women and children (PTPPA) at the village or urban village level are carried out by the Village or Urban Village Post.
2. The establishment and facilitation of the operation of the Village or Urban Village Post is the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus Organization in charge of women's empowerment and child protection affairs and the Village Government.
3. The Village or Urban Village Post as referred to in paragraph (1) is established with the tasks of:
a. providing complaint services;
b. providing counseling services;
c. providing assistance services;
d. providing referral services to the Sub-district and Regency Integrated Service Centers and to other related parties;
e. providing social reintegration services;
f. coordinating case handling;
g. collecting data on case handling at the village or urban village level;
h. conducting prevention at the village or urban village level.
4. The Integrated Service Center for the protection of women and children (PPTPPA) as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of;
a. village or urban village government;
b. health clinics and/or village or urban village midwives;
c. family empowerment and welfare;
d. community organizations;
e. religious organizations;
f. community-based service institutions;
g. women's organizations; and h. religious leaders, community leaders and customary leaders.
5. The structure of the Village or Urban Village Post as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
a. advisors;
b. chairperson;
c. secretary; and
d. divisions.
6. The structure of the Village or Urban Village Post as referred to in paragraph (1) is established through a decree of the village head or urban village head.

CHAPTER X GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 27

1. Guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence is conducted by the regency government and village government.
2. Guidance and supervision by the regency government as referred to in paragraph (1) includes the implementation of protection for women and children at the regency, sub-district and village or kelurahan levels.
3. Guidance and supervision by the village government as referred to in paragraph (1) includes the implementation of protection for women and children at the village level.
4. Guidance and supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a.guidelines and service operational standards;
b.routine coordination;
c. technical guidance and training, provision of facilities and infrastructure and human resources;
d.monitoring and evaluation;
e. reporting.
5. Guidance and supervision is conducted at least once a year and its implementation is the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus Organization in charge of women's empowerment and Child Protection affairs and the Regional Inspectorate.
6. The results of the implementation of guidance and supervision as referred to in paragraph (5) are reported to the Regent and submitted to related Regional Apparatus Organizations, Village Governments, the community and the business sector.
